   "title": "Scouts slain: the army forms a square",
   "time": "Morning, 9 June",
   "narrative": "Near Carrhae the Roman reconnaissance screen finds its scouts dead and a large body of cavalry approaching. The army panics. Cassius advises the old order, legions in the centre and horse on both wings. Crassus agrees, then thinks again and closes the army into a hollow square, twelve cohorts to a side. A square cannot be turned, but it can barely manoeuvre. Light troops and cavalry are shown behind it here; Plutarch places them within.",
